.TH DATE 1
.SH NAME
date \- print or set the date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdate [\fB\-qsu\fR] [[\fIMMDDYY\fR]\fIhhmm\fR[\fIss\fR][\fBZ\fR] | \fIMMDDCCYYhhmmss\fR[\fBZ\fR]] [\fI+format\fR]\fR
.br
.de FL
.TP
\\fB\\$1\\fR
\\$2
..
.de EX
.TP 20
\\fB\\$1\\fR
# \\$2
..
.SH OPTIONS
.FL "\-q" "Read the date from \fIstdin\fR"
.FL "\-s" "Set the time (implicit for \fB\-q\fR or a date string)"
.FL "\-u" "Print (and read) the date as UTC instead of local time"
.FL "\-t" "Use this number of seconds instead of current time"
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX "date" "Print the date and time"
.EX "date 0221921610" "Set date to Feb 21, 1992 at 4:10 p.m. local time"
.EX "date 01192038031400Z" "Set date to Jan 19, 2038 at 03:14:00 UTC"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The system clock keeps UTC; local time comes from the
.B TZ
environment variable (a POSIX rule such as
.BR CET\-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3 ;
none: UTC).
With the \fB\-q\fR flag or a numeric argument,
.I date
sets the time and date.  The date given is local time, or UTC with
\fB\-u\fR or a trailing \fBZ\fR; the clock always receives UTC.
.I MMDDYY
refers to the month, day, and year (a two-digit year is 1970\-2069;
.I MMDDCCYYhhmmss
gives the century too);
.I hhmmss
refers to the hour, minute and second.
Each field must be exactly two digits, no more and no less.
The clock keeps times from 1970 to 2106-02-07; others give
"The clock cannot keep that time".
.I date
always display the date and time, with the default format for the system.
The \fB\-u\fR flag requests UTC instead of local time.
A format may be specified with a + followed by a printf-like string with
the following options:
.ta 0.25i
.nf
.PP
	%%  % character
	%A  Name of the day
	%B  Name of the month
	%D  mm/dd/yy
	%H  Decimal hour on 2 digits
	%I  Decimal hour modulo 12 on 2 digits
	%M  Decimal minute on 2 digits
	%S  Decimal seconds on 2 digits
	%T  HH:MM:SS
	%U  Decimal week number, Sunday being first day of week
	%W  Decimal week number, Monday being first day of week
	%X  Same as %T
	%Y  Decimal year on 4 digits
	%Z  Time Zone (if any)
	%a  Abbreviated name of the day
	%b  Abbreviated name of the month
	%c  Appropriate date & time (default format)
	%d  Decimal day of the month on 2 digits
	%e  Same as %d, but a space replaces leading 0
	%h  Same as %b
	%j  Decimal dey of the year on 3 digits
	%m  Decimal month on 2 digits
	%n  Newline character
	%p  AM or PM
	%r  12-hour clock time with AM/PM
	%s  Number of seconds since the epoch
	%t  Tab character
	%w  Decimal day of the week (0=Sunday)
	%x  Same as %D
	%y  Decimal year on 2 digits
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR time (2),
.BR ctime (3),
.BR readclock (8).
